---
<BlogLayout
  title="Size Grading for Full-Bust Bras: Where Ranges Break"
  description="Why scaling a 34B pattern to a 42F does not work, how grading increments should change above a D cup, and what to specify so your full-bust sizes actually fit."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">Full-Bust Grading</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="aspect-[16/10] overflow-hidden bg-cream-dark mb-8">
      <Picture src="blog-size-grading-hero.webp" alt="Full-bust lace bra with wider band and deeper cup construction" cls="w-full h-full object-cover" priority />
    </div>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">November 8, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">Size Grading for Full-Bust Bras: Where Ranges Break</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        Almost every bad full-bust bra we have been asked to fix had the same origin story: someone took
        a 34B block that fitted beautifully, ran it through a grading table, and extended it to 42F. The
        34B still fits. The 42F is a disaster. This is not a workmanship problem — it is that grading a
        bra is not linear, and the assumption that it is has a specific point where it fails.
      </p>

      <h2>What grading actually does</h2>
      <p>
        Grading takes a base size pattern and applies an increment per size step — for example, +5cm
        underbust circumference and +2.5cm across the cup depth per step. Done well, this works
        acceptably across a contiguous band of sizes. Done blindly, it silently assumes that the
        <em>relationship</em> between components stays constant. It does not.
      </p>

      <h2>The D-cup boundary</h2>
      <p>
        In our experience the practical break point is around a D cup, and it breaks in several ways at
        once:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cup weight grows faster than cup volume.</strong> A larger cup holds more fabric mass, which means the same wire gauge and the same strap width are now under-spec for the load.</li>
        <li><strong>The wire needs to change shape, not just scale.</strong> A scaled-up small wire gets taller and wider but is still the wrong curve — a full bust typically needs a deeper, more rounded wire rather than a proportionally enlarged one.</li>
        <li><strong>The band must carry more.</strong> Support has to move from the straps back to the band, which means a wider elastic and often a third hook.</li>
        <li><strong>Strap placement shifts.</strong> Scaling keeps the apex position proportional, but a full bust needs the apex lower and the strap attachment further inboard or the cup tips forward.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you specify nothing else for a full-bust range, specify these four. They are the difference
        between a range that fits at the top end and one that only photographs well.
      </p>

      <h2>The grading table is not the product</h2>
      <p>
        There is a second, subtler failure mode: brands buy a grading table off the shelf — a standard
        increment set used across the industry — and treat it as a specification. A grading table is a
        starting point calibrated to a particular block, on a particular last, for a particular market.
        Applied to a different block it produces sizes that are internally consistent and externally
        wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        The test is simple and cheap: get a fit model in your target top size and your target bottom size,
        not just the middle. Grading errors are almost invisible at the base size and glaring two steps
        away from it.
      </p>

      <h2>What a proper full-bust spec contains</h2>
      <p>
        When we develop a full-bust range, the specification carries more than measurements:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Wire spec per size band</strong> — not one wire scaled, but two or three wire geometries across the range.</li>
        <li><strong>Band elastic width per size band</strong> — commonly stepping up at the DD and again at the G boundary.</li>
        <li><strong>Hook count per size band</strong> — 2-hook at the bottom of the range, 3-hook from DD upward.</li>
        <li><strong>Strap width and stretch grade per size band</strong> — a 12mm strap on a 40F is a customer complaint waiting to happen.</li>
        <li><strong>Cup construction changes</strong> — a cup that is cut-and-sew at 34B may need a stabilised side panel or a different seam layout at 40G.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is why full-bust development costs more. It is not arbitrary; it is genuinely more pattern
        work, and the factories that quote it as if it is the same job as a core-size range are either
        planning to skip the work or have not done it before.
      </p>

      <h2>Testing that catches grading errors before production</h2>
      <p>
        Three checks we run that catch most problems at sample stage rather than at the customer:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Fit on the boundary sizes, on a body.</strong> Not a mannequin. A dress form will hold a
        bad fit quietly; a fit model will not. Test the smallest and largest size in the range, and test
        the size where you switched wire geometry.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Strap load test.</strong> Hang a weighted form from the straps for 24 hours and measure
        elastic recovery. Straps that are under-spec for a full-bust cup fail this before they fail on a
        customer.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Band recovery after wash cycles.</strong> Ten cycles is usually enough to separate
        adequate elastic from inadequate. Measure the band length before and after; anything beyond
        roughly 3% permanent stretch is a problem that will grow.
      </p>

      <h2>The commercial reality</h2>
      <p>
        Full-bust is one of the few genuinely underserved segments in intimate apparel, and it is
        underserved specifically because it is hard to make well. That is an opportunity, but only for
        brands willing to fund the pattern work properly. If the budget is not there for a real full-bust
        development, the better commercial decision is usually to cap the range at a DD and do the small
        sizes excellently — rather than extend to a G and be quietly returned by every customer above a
        D.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
